preConditionHook:
// GRIMES'S "STOP A", INSIDE THE TESTED LEVEL. The stop sits stopFraction of
// the way from the entry price DOWN TOWARD the tested range extreme, so the
// exit happens before the mass of resting stop orders sitting at the level
// (G04-09).
//
// NOT StopFractionOfSignalBarRisk: identical arithmetic, a different FAR
// ANCHOR and a different DIRECTION of measurement -- that rule measures up
// from the SIGNAL BAR'S low, this one down from the ENTRY PRICE toward the
// tested RANGE extreme. Complementary; both kept.
//
// THE LEVEL IS THE ONE-BAR-SHIFTED WINDOW EXTREME (merge decision 7), captured
// ONCE on ctx.BarsSinceEntry() == 0 -- VERIFIED as 0 on the fill bar and as
// restarting through a reversal, which a ctx.MarketPosition() == 0 reset would
// miss because a reversal never passes through flat. Capturing rather than
// re-indexing by the position's age holds "reaches back" at levelLookback.
//
// NO ARM FLAG IS NEEDED: BarsSinceEntry() is 0 on every flat bar too, so the
// captured level is refreshed right up to the fill and is never read stale.
// The MarketPosition guard is load-bearing anyway -- EntryPrice() is 0.0 while
// flat and a back-adjusted series carries an arbitrary offset.
//
// The window mirrors EasyLanguage's Extremes(), which Lowest()/Highest() call:
// seeded at the first bar of the window, scanning the rest, STRICT comparison
// so a tie keeps the earliest bar. Extremes is EL SOURCE, so its comparisons
// carry the relational tolerance -- el_lt/el_gt, as in Breakout.
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry, never a hook-scope
// declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
//
// Deepest bar touched: levelLookback.
if (el_eq((double)ctx.BarsSinceEntry(), 0.0)) {
    levelLow = low[1];
    levelHigh = high[1];
    for (int i = 2; i <= (int)levelLookback; ++i) {
        if (el_lt(low[i], levelLow)) levelLow = low[i];
        if (el_gt(high[i], levelHigh)) levelHigh = high[i];
    }
}
